40	/**
41	 * The figures a sheet shows: each person's claimed and due-total, and the sheet's totals. A change takes a
42	 * {@link #snapshot} before it and calls {@link #recordChanges} after it, so the audit gets one ROLLUP line per person
43	 * whose figures (claimed, due-total, overdue-total) moved (in list order) and a TOTALS line when the sheet's totals moved.
44	 */
45	@Service
46	public class RollupService {
47	
48	    /**
49	     * One person's figures; a null due-total is n/a. The overdue-total sums their overdue claims' dues. The
50	     * settled-total sums the figures of their counted settled claims that correct nothing, and the overdue count is how many of their counted
51	     * claims are overdue (whether or not they have a figure). The held-total sums the parts they hold of others'
52	     * shared claims (null when they hold none), which their due-total counts. The split settled-total and open-total
53	     * are the figures of their counted settled claims (corrections included) and of their counted open and pending
54	     * ones, before the cap, as their row shows them; the cut total is their cuts of the pools' pots.
55	     */
56	    public record Figures(String name, BigDecimal claimed, BigDecimal dueTotal, BigDecimal overdueTotal,
57	            BigDecimal settledTotal, int overdueCount, BigDecimal heldTotal, BigDecimal splitSettledTotal,
58	            BigDecimal openTotal, BigDecimal cutTotal) {
59	
60	        /**
61	         * The person's score: their settled-total divided by 100, minus 5 for each of their overdue claims, rounded
62	         * half to even to a whole number.
63	         */
64	        public BigDecimal score() {
65	            return settledTotal.subtract(BigDecimal.valueOf(500L * overdueCount))
66	                    .divide(BigDecimal.valueOf(100)).setScale(0, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN);
67	        }
68	    }
